E('magna carta date|runnymede', 'Runnymede is the meadow beside the River Thames where Magna Carta was sealed.', 'It happened in June 1215.', 'It lies near Windsor, west of London.', 'A memorial there was built by the American Bar Association in 1957.', 'A memorial to President Kennedy stands nearby.')
E('irish famine|great famine|potato famine', 'The Great Famine was a period of mass starvation in Ireland.', 'It lasted from 1845 to 1852.', 'A disease called potato blight destroyed the main food crop.', 'About a million people died.', 'Over a million emigrated.', 'Food was still exported from Ireland during the famine.', 'Ireland’s population has still not returned to its pre-famine level.')
E('holodomor', 'The Holodomor was a man-made famine in Soviet Ukraine.', 'It took place in 1932 and 1933.', 'Stalin’s collectivisation and grain seizures caused it.', 'Millions of Ukrainians died.', 'Its name means “death by hunger”.', 'Many countries recognise it as a genocide.')
E('armenian genocide', 'The Armenian genocide was the mass killing of Armenians by the Ottoman government.', 'It took place mainly in 1915 and 1916.', 'Around one million or more Armenians died.', 'Many died in death marches into the Syrian desert.', 'It is commemorated on 24 April.')
E('apartheid south africa|apartheid laws', 'Apartheid was a system of racial segregation in South Africa.', 'It was enforced from 1948 to the early 1990s.', 'People were classified by race.', 'Black South Africans were forced into separate areas and denied votes.', 'The Sharpeville massacre of 1960 drew world attention.', 'International sanctions and protests helped end it.')
E('woodstock', 'Woodstock was a famous music festival in New York State.', 'It took place in August 1969.', 'About 400,000 people attended.', 'Performers included Jimi Hendrix and Janis Joplin.', 'It became a symbol of the 1960s counterculture.')
E('moon landing anniversary|first moonwalk', 'The first moonwalk began on 20 July 1969 (21 July in UTC).', 'Neil Armstrong stepped out first, followed by Buzz Aldrin.', 'They collected about 22 kilograms of samples.', 'Their footprints are still there, as there is no wind on the Moon.')
E('league of nations', 'The League of Nations was an international organisation created after the First World War.', 'It was founded in 1920.', 'It aimed to prevent future wars.', 'The United States never joined.', 'It failed to stop aggression in the 1930s.', 'It was replaced by the United Nations in 1946.')
E('prohibition', 'Prohibition was a nationwide ban on alcohol in the United States.', 'It lasted from 1920 to 1933.', 'It led to illegal bars called speakeasies.', 'Gangsters like Al Capone grew rich smuggling alcohol.', 'It was repealed by the 21st Amendment.')
E('al capone', 'Al Capone was an American gangster in Chicago.', 'He lived from 1899 to 1947.', 'He grew rich during Prohibition.', 'He was linked to the St Valentine’s Day Massacre of 1929.', 'He was finally jailed for tax evasion.', 'He spent time in Alcatraz prison.')
E('alcatraz', 'Alcatraz is an island prison in San Francisco Bay.', 'It operated as a federal prison from 1934 to 1963.', 'It held notorious criminals like Al Capone.', 'In 1962 three men escaped and were never found.', 'It is now a museum.')
E('jack the ripper', 'Jack the Ripper was an unidentified serial killer in London.', 'The murders took place in 1888.', 'He killed at least five women in Whitechapel.', 'He was never caught.', 'Many suspects have been proposed.')
E('roaring twenties|1920s', 'The Roaring Twenties was a decade of prosperity and cultural change.', 'It refers to the 1920s, mainly in the US and Europe.', 'Jazz music became hugely popular.', 'Young women called flappers wore short dresses and bobbed hair.', 'It ended with the Wall Street Crash of 1929.')
E('hippies|1960s|swinging sixties', 'The 1960s was a decade of social upheaval and cultural change.', 'It ran from 1960 to 1969.', 'It saw the civil rights movement and protests against the Vietnam War.', 'The hippie counterculture rejected mainstream values.', 'London became a centre of fashion and music.')
E('statue of zeus|colossus of rhodes', 'The Colossus of Rhodes was a giant bronze statue of the sun god Helios.', 'It was completed around 280 BC.', 'It stood about 33 metres tall.', 'An earthquake toppled it in 226 BC.', 'It probably did not straddle the harbour, as often shown.')
E('lighthouse of alexandria', 'The Lighthouse of Alexandria was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.', 'It was built in the 3rd century BC.', 'It may have been over 100 metres tall.', 'It stood on the island of Pharos.', 'Earthquakes destroyed it by the 14th century.')
E('rosetta stone', 'The Rosetta Stone is a stone slab that helped decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs.', 'It was carved in 196 BC and found in 1799.', 'It has the same text in hieroglyphs, Demotic and Greek.', 'French soldiers found it in Egypt.', 'Jean-François Champollion deciphered hieroglyphs in 1822.', 'It is in the British Museum.')
E('dead sea scrolls', 'The Dead Sea Scrolls are ancient Jewish manuscripts.', 'They were found from 1947 in caves near the Dead Sea.', 'They date from about the 3rd century BC to the 1st century AD.', 'They include the oldest known copies of Hebrew Bible texts.', 'A Bedouin shepherd found the first ones.')
E('domesday book', 'The Domesday Book is a great survey of England.', 'It was completed in 1086 for William the Conqueror.', 'It recorded land, people and animals.', 'It helped William assess taxes.', 'It is kept at the National Archives in London.')
E('hadrian’s wall|hadrians wall', 'Hadrian’s Wall is a Roman wall across northern England.', 'Building began in AD 122.', 'It was ordered by Emperor Hadrian.', 'It ran about 118 kilometres from coast to coast.', 'Forts and milecastles lined it.', 'Long stretches still stand.')
E('cuneiform tablets|clay tablets', 'Clay tablets were the main writing surface in ancient Mesopotamia.', 'They were used from about 3200 BC.', 'Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into wet clay.', 'Hundreds of thousands survive.', 'Some are school exercises and even complaints about bad copper.')
E('hammurabi code examples', 'Hammurabi’s Code set punishments based on social rank.', 'It dates from around 1754 BC.', 'A builder whose house collapsed and killed the owner could be executed.', 'It also set wages and prices.')
E('trade unions|labour movement', 'Trade unions are organisations of workers who bargain together.', 'They grew during the Industrial Revolution.', 'They campaigned for shorter hours and safer workplaces.', 'The eight-hour day was a major goal.', 'May Day honours workers in many countries.')
E('women in science history', 'Women made major scientific discoveries throughout history, often unrecognised.', 'Examples range from Hypatia in the 4th century to Marie Curie in the 20th.', 'Ada Lovelace wrote the first computer program.', 'Rosalind Franklin’s work revealed the shape of DNA.', 'Chien-Shiung Wu proved a key law of physics wrong in 1956.', 'Lise Meitner helped explain nuclear fission.')
E('lise meitner', 'Lise Meitner was an Austrian-Swedish physicist.', 'She lived from 1878 to 1968.', 'She helped discover nuclear fission.', 'She fled Nazi Germany in 1938.', 'Her colleague Otto Hahn alone received the Nobel Prize.', 'Element 109, meitnerium, is named after her.')
E('chien-shiung wu|chien shiung wu', 'Chien-Shiung Wu was a Chinese-American physicist.', 'She lived from 1912 to 1997.', 'Her 1956 experiment showed that nature does not always treat left and right the same.', 'She worked on the Manhattan Project.', 'She was called the “First Lady of Physics”.')
